PT_1: MAKE OPTIONS

- Main Makefile

None:
	compiles a dynamic executable for the native platform
		eg. 32 bit, 64 bit, ARM Linux

win_9x_DOS:
	compiles a static executable for Windows:
			95, 98, 98 SE, Millenium Edition, NT 4.0 with Service Pack 3.0, 2000, XP, Vista, 7, 10, 11
			64/32 bit, will not work on ARM and 16 bit systems.
	Read the Makefile for instructions how to make a dynamic executable. (Comment out 1 line actually)
	DOS refeers to the fact that Win 95/98 are just 32 bit DOS, it will not work on 16 bit DOS systems. (MSDOS 7.0 for 32 bit)

- Amiga Makefile

None:
	compiles a static executable for Amiga OS (3.1), requires GPU drivers.
	I didn't check it cuz I am too stupid to set up the emulator for my needs tho-
	You have to trust me on word with this one.

PT_2: UNDERSTANDING FILE STRUCTURE

| main folder
	consists of sub folders, game files, and files for the native architectures.
| specific folders (eg. amiga/)
	consists of a special Makefile, install instructions and a precompiled library for that system
| release/ folder
	consists of precompiled binaries I put here for convinience.

PT_3: NAMING CONVENTION

native - no tweaks needed, you can use make to build the game on this architecture with no tweaks.
cross compiles - building this on these would be nightmare so I provided Makefiles to cross compile from linux to these architectures to get a clean executable.
ARCH - architecture (eg. x86_64)
COMPAT - list of compatible systems
LEGAL - license
bloat - unnecesary things
